Title: DECK 52
Credit: Pilot — Episode 101
Author: "The Door"
Source: Consequences of Failure — series pilot teleplay, draft v1 (2026-07-10)
Notes: Companion to DECK_52_series_bible.md. Canon per ../film/CANON_NOTES.md. TV act structure (teaser + 5 acts). The feature covers the full origin; this pilot leans into the ENSEMBLE and the ENGINE — the Court, the loss, the secret — and uses origin only as flashback seasoning. Cold open shared with the feature by design (franchise iconography).
Draft date: 2026-07-10
Contact: Machine King Media

====

> **TEASER**

FADE IN:

EXT. PACIFIC OCEAN - NIGHT

Black water. A United States aircraft carrier, a city asleep on the sea.

SUPER: "USS CARL VINSON — 200 MILES OFF GUAM"

INT. CARL VINSON - AFT ELECTRICAL WORKSHOP - CONTINUOUS

A young CORPSMAN rounds a junction box — and stops.

PETTY OFFICER DANA REYES (26) sits upright against the steel. Hands folded. Uniform pressed. Like a woman waiting for a bus.

He kneels. Two fingers to her throat. Nothing. He tilts her head to start compressions —

— her jaw opens, and a thread of PALE SMOKE rises and HANGS in the still air. It does not disperse.

He stops.

CORPSMAN
(barely)
Corpsman up. Corpsman *up—*

INT. CARL VINSON - MEDICAL - LATER

An X-ray on a lightbox: a chest cavity cooked to carbon. LT. CMDR. HOLLIS (50s) stares.

He crosses to the body. Unzips the bag a foot. Reyes's face — untouched, serene.

HOLLIS
(to a corpsman; quiet)
Nothing touched her. She's been cremated from the inside, and her collar isn't even singed.
(beat)
Get me a phone that reaches someone who isn't us.

On a steel tray: the smoke, trapped under an inverted jar. Still hanging. Still there.

SMASH TO BLACK.

A single PLAYING CARD flips down onto the black — face down.

MAIN TITLE: DECK 52

> **END OF TEASER**

====

> **ACT ONE**

INT. CRYSTAL CITY - FEDERAL BUILDING - ELEVATOR BANK - DAY

NAOMI PIERCE (44) — diamond pin, scalpel-precise — walks out of a closed door and into an empty corridor, materials clutched to her chest, jaw locked. She just lost. We don't need the meeting; we can read it on her.

She stabs the elevator call button. Waits. And in the polished steel of the doors, we see the meeting she's replaying — a 10-SECOND FLASH, no audio, just image:

FLASH — the unmarked conference room. Two enormous photographs of Dana Reyes. Pierce mid-sentence, telling an admiral the truth. And across the table a man in a black cowboy hat sliding a tablet down its length — TODD "SHOGUN" BENCHLEY (54) — and the admiral reaching for a pen while Pierce is still talking.

BACK TO — the elevator doors. Pierce's own reflection. The doors open on the man himself.

BENCHLEY
(settling the hat on)
Going down?

PIERCE
(stepping in beside him; flat)
There's another elevator.

BENCHLEY
There is. But you want to ask me something, and you're the type that hates wanting things.

The doors close on them. Two professionals, alone.

PIERCE
I told them the truth. That nobody on Earth knows what killed that girl. You told them you did. You *don't.*

BENCHLEY
I told them where the chemistry isn't from. That's not nothing.

PIERCE
It's a guess wearing a lab coat.
(beat)
When your prep team ran my brief — did they flag what I left out of it? Or did you catch it yourself?

BENCHLEY
(the ghost of a smile; genuine respect)
I caught the *way* you left it out. Cleanest work I've lost to in a year.
(the doors open; he steps out; turns back)
For what it's worth, Ms. Pierce — the honest one should've won. Tell your boss a soldier said so. He'll know I mean it.

He's gone. Pierce rides down alone, turning that over.

INT. DECK 52 TOWER - EXECUTIVE FLOOR - DAY

Glass and steel. Four inlaid SUIT SIGILS mark four wings. Pierce strides in, still hot from the loss — and walks straight into the standing weather system that is this company's leadership.

DR. MARA VANCE (58), Queen of Hearts, knitting as she walks, needles never looking down. IDRIS KANÉ (49), King of Clubs, in a linen shirt with a soldier's spine. DR. LENA CHO (41), Queen of Spades, reading a tablet and everyone at once.

KANÉ
(to Pierce; already knows)
We lost it. Of course we lost it. Benchley told them a bedtime story and we told them the truth, and the truth doesn't sign contracts.
(beat)
And the Dealer will do what the Dealer does. Nothing. Beautifully.

VANCE
(needles clicking)
It's not a war, Idris. It's a counter-terror contracting firm with excellent dental.

KANÉ
(flat)
Mara. You've buried more of our people than I have. You know exactly what it is.

The needles STOP. The floor goes quiet — everyone's learned what that means.

VANCE
(quiet; dangerous)
I know what it costs. That's a different expertise than yours.
(resuming; brisk)
And whatever it is, it's a thing a grieving man built and then walked away from, and the four of us run it toward four different futures while he looks at a photograph. *That's* the problem in this building. Not Benchley.

CHO
(not looking up from the tablet; sublimely calm)
The problem is everyone keeps calling it a problem instead of a market.
(finally looks up)
That sailor's chemistry doesn't exist. Which means someone is *making* it. Which means there's a supplier. I don't want the contract, Naomi. I want a tissue sample and forty minutes.

PIERCE
(carefully; she knows something they don't)
FIST has the body. And federal custody. Nobody's getting near that sample.

CHO
(returning to the tablet; mild)
Bodies leak, Naomi. Everything leaks. That's practically my whole field.

She walks off. Kané and Vance exchange a look — the hawk and the healer, united for one second by shared unease about the engineer. And Pierce watches all three, holding the shape of a company whose founder they've written off as absent.

INT. DECK 52 TOWER - CHARLES'S OFFICE - DAY

CHARLES SAPPHIRE (40s) at the glass. Muted on the wall: a cable-news graphic — a fanned deck over a world map. CHYRON: "WHO IS DECK 52?" Pierce enters — she's earned the no-knock — and sets down her case with controlled fury.

PIERCE
We lost the sailor. To a *guess.* And downstairs your Court is deciding what this company is without you, because — their words — you'll do nothing, beautifully.
(beat)
I don't need absolution, Charles. I did right. I need a decision. That chemistry doesn't exist on Earth and it's about to disappear into Vaughn Galloway's vault.

CHARLES
(not turning; mild)
Let the Court talk. A company that's understood is a company that's finished. I'll survive being sensationalized. I'd never survive being *explained.*
(beat)
And I let them believe I do nothing because the day I walk that floor and pick a side, the losing three unite against me and I lose the company in a year. My stillness is the only thing keeping the peace.

PIERCE
(sharp)
Stillness isn't a strategy, Charles. It's a place people put their grief and call it wisdom.

That lands. Charles turns. And for the first time, the stillness lifts entirely — and what's underneath is not a mourner. He crosses to the desk, opens a drawer, and takes out a single unmarked burner phone — one he clearly does not use for ordinary things.

CHARLES
In four days Vaughn will stand on a stage and announce he's holding a piece of the future. The hour he does, every service on Earth comes for it, and it vanishes under a mountain, and we meet what's coming a decade behind.
(beat)
I am not going to let the single most important object on this planet belong to the vainest man on it.

He dials. One number. One ring.

CHARLES (CONT'D)
(into the phone; quiet, absolute)
It's me. FIST sent tissue duplicates to a Santiago lab for the outside confirm. I don't want them stolen. I want them *lost.* A refrigeration failure. A clerical error. A sad little accident that embarrasses no one.
(listens)
A viable sample on Dr. Cho's bench by Friday, and it looks like weather.
(beat)
And Marc — if it goes wrong, it goes wrong on me. Not the company. Me.

He hangs up. Sets the burner back in the drawer. Pierce stares at him — she has just watched the oracle become a general, and it unsettles her more than the stillness ever did.

PIERCE
(quietly)
...How long could you always do that? And let me think you couldn't?

CHARLES
(the ghost of something)
I pick the cards I turn over, Naomi. Today I turned over one.

A beat. And now Pierce plays hers. The reason she really came up.

PIERCE
Then turn over this one too.
(beat; watching him)
I traced our targets. Eight years of "coincidental" hits. I overlaid them on the network that ran the garment-district attack — the one that killed—
(she won't say Emily's name)
Node for node, Charles. It matches. This company was never counter-terror. It's one funeral, wearing a balance sheet.

Silence. Charles does not flinch, does not confess, does not deny. That, itself, is the answer.

CHARLES
(finally; gentle)
And what did you do with it? The file.

PIERCE
Encrypted it. Filed it where only I'll look. Not to hold a knife to you.
(beat)
Somebody in this company should know the true north — in case the man who set it ever stops pointing.

A long, loaded beat. Two people acknowledging the secret at the center of everything without either of them saying it aloud.

CHARLES
(quietly)
House rules.

PIERCE
(the ghost of a nod)
House rules.

She leaves. Charles stands alone. Looks at the red-dress photograph — and, for the first time on screen, his eyes MOVE, tracking something we can't see, faster than grief.

He is not mourning. He is reading.

SMASH TO:

INT. MIT - DORM COMMON ROOM - NIGHT (FLASHBACK - 18 YEARS AGO)

SUPER: "EIGHTEEN YEARS AGO."

A low-stakes dorm POKER GAME. Five students, a lamp, cheap beer. YOUNG CHARLES (18) — glasses, guarded — has a laptop open beside him, running an early build labeled BRAINTRAIN. YOUNG VAUGHN (18) — lanky, silver already threading his black hair — deals, chatty, winning.

VAUGHN
Sapphire's down forty bucks and building software instead of playing. That's the whole man, right there.

CHARLES
(mild; not looking up)
BrainTrain reads a book in nine seconds and gives me the argument. I pointed it at the game.
(beat)
It doesn't read the cards, Vaughn. It reads *you.* Micro-expression, breath, the half-second before you bluff.

VAUGHN
(grinning)
Sure it does.

Charles turns the laptop a few degrees — so only we, and he, can see the overlay: on the webcam feed of the table, faint tags float over each player. PULSE. PUPIL. TELL: HIGH. Over Vaughn: BLUFF — 0.91.

CHARLES
(quietly)
All in.

VAUGHN
(delighted)
You're bluffing—

CHARLES
No. You are.

He lays down nothing special. Vaughn, cocky, calls — and loses with a busted draw. The table groans. Charles rakes the pot without pleasure. He's staring at the laptop like it bit him.

VAUGHN (CONT'D)
(lighting up, seeing dollar signs)
Do you understand what you just — Charles. We could sell that. We could sell that to *everyone.*

CHARLES
(closing the laptop; unsettled)
That's the problem. I turned it on a game to be clever. And now I know I can turn it on a *person.*
(beat)
A man who can always see the other man's hand shouldn't be trusted to deal. That's not an advantage. That's a temptation.

VAUGHN
(clapping his shoulder; already gone to money)
It's an advantage AND a temptation. It can be two things.

Charles looks at the closed laptop. At his own hands. A boy who has just met the thing he'll spend his life both using and fearing.

SMASH BACK TO:

INT. DECK 52 TOWER - CHARLES'S OFFICE - PRESENT

Charles, alone, at the desk — the same hands, twenty years older. He turns the red-dress photograph face-down. Gentle. Final.

And a VOICE speaks — low, warm, meant for no one but him. A cadence that will, much later, mean everything.

THE VOICE (V.O.)
You turned over a card today. It felt good. That should worry you.

CHARLES
(barely; to the voice)
It does.

THE VOICE (V.O.)
He'll come here. You know he will. The only question left is which of us you'll be when he does.

Charles doesn't answer. He looks at the face-down photograph, and does not turn it back over.

> **END OF ACT ONE**

====

> **ACT TWO**

INT. FIST HEADQUARTERS - VAUGHN'S OFFICE - DAY

The opposite of Charles's austerity: glass, toys, a first-edition chess set, a framed LOBBY CARD of his father's best film hung slightly crooked. VAUGHN GALLOWAY (40s) — charm as a load-bearing structure — pours two bourbons though it's early. Benchley declines his with a look.

VAUGHN
We won the most important contract in the history of private defense and you're standing there like I ran over a dog.

BENCHLEY
I stood in that room and told an admiral we'd seen this twice. We've seen it once.

VAUGHN
We saw it once and *inferred* the fisherman. Inference is just a fact that hasn't finished getting dressed.

BENCHLEY
(not smiling)
It was a lie, Vaughn. A good one. I'm very good at delivering your good lies. I'd like to know, before I deliver the next one, what we actually have.

Vaughn's charm dims a notch. He crosses to a wall safe, thumbs it open, and takes out a SEALED EVIDENCE JAR — the smoke — and a thin folder marked with FIST's crest and a red band.

VAUGHN
(quieter; the salesman receding)
What we have, Todd, is the first physical proof — court-grade, isotope-verified — that something not from this planet reached out and switched off a healthy young woman on the deck of a United States warship.

He sets the jar on the desk between them. The smoke turns, slow, in its glass.

VAUGHN (CONT'D)
(looking at it, not at Benchley)
Everyone I've ever told about the sky has laughed. My father told the whole country, and they buried him in a season.
(beat)
Nobody's laughing at this jar.

Benchley looks at the jar a long moment. This is the first time we — and he — understand that Vaughn's clowning has always been armor over something real.

BENCHLEY
Who else knows what's in that folder?

VAUGHN
You. Me. Three of my scientists who think it's a chemical-weapons case.
(beat)
And the one man on Earth who'll believe me without laughing — who I can't call, because I made sure of that on a talk show with a drink in my hand.

Benchley absorbs this — the shape of a twenty-year wound.

BENCHLEY
Sapphire.

VAUGHN
(the charm snapping back like stage lights; too bright)
Drink your bourbon, Todd. We're about to be the most important company on Earth. Try to enjoy the part before it gets hard.

INT. DECK 52 TOWER - CHO'S CLEANROOM - DAY

Glass, white light, the low hum of expensive machines. CHO watches a RoboForge rig: a slurry of gray nanite mass and sand rising, self-assembling into a perfect, functional DRONE. It hovers.

CHO
(soft; to the drone; genuine affection)
There you are. Good. Good.

The drone flies four feet — stutters — and COLLAPSES into a heap of sand. Cho doesn't flinch; she's watched this a thousand times.

MADDOX (late 30s) — Ace of Spades, unfailingly polite — hands her a coffee.

CHO (CONT'D)
Ninety-one seconds. Up from eighty-eight.
(to the sand)
You don't die because you're badly made. You die because you have to *eat* to build, and there's nothing on Earth you can digest that gives back more than it costs. I feed you sand, you get ninety seconds. I feed you an engine block, you get five minutes and then you've eaten the engine.

MADDOX
So stop feeding it dead things. Find it something that eats — and breeds — on its own.

Cho goes very still. Looks at him. Looks at the whiteboard, where POWER is circled in red. She draws an arrow from it to a new phrase — FUEL THAT REPLICATES — and then, smaller, the impossible thing that would solve feeding and power in one stroke: a self-multiplying, metal-devouring appetite that does not exist. She writes it and stares:

METAL-EATER.

CHO
(quiet; electric)
...Maddox. Remind me to promote you before you say the next thing. Because the next thing gets us both killed.

MADDOX
(already leaving)
Bit late, Doctor.

She stares at the two words. Somewhere out past the window, unknown to her, something is already on its way that will answer them.

INT. DECK 52 TOWER - HEARTS CLINIC - DAY

Recovery beds. Machines. VANCE moves between two patients. In one bed: a YOUNG OPERATIVE, sedated — and his arm glows faintly, wrong, under the skin. Vance lifts the dressing. What's underneath is not a wound. It's a *build* that's failing.

At the far end of the hall, a door: "INITIATIVE — VOLUNTEERS BY APPOINTMENT."

JACK "SULLY" SULLIVAN (53), Jack of Hearts, ex-corpsman, joins her — profane warmth, a file in hand.

SULLY
Third failing graft this quarter. Spades says the interface is "within tolerance."

VANCE
(re-dressing the arm; flat)
Spades would say that about a house fire.
(beat)
Whose signature cleared him for the Initiative?

SULLY
(reads; grimaces)
...Yours. Eleven months ago. Before you started reading the fine print.

That lands on Vance like a slap. She looks down the hall at that door.

VANCE
(quietly)
I signed this boy up for what's eating him.

SULLY
You signed a form somebody put in a stack of forty. That's how they do it, Mara. Nobody decides to build a monster. They just each sign one form.

VANCE
(a decision arriving; the needles-stopping calm)
I want every Initiative candidate routed through my desk before Spades touches them. Personally. Starting now.

SULLY
Cho will fight you.

VANCE
Cho will *lose* to me, one form at a time, until the man who drew the line remembers he drew it.
(beat)
Where is Charles, Sully?

Sully just looks at her. They both know: in his office. Looking at a photograph. Not walking the floor.

INT. DECK 52 TOWER - HALLWAY OUTSIDE THE INITIATIVE - CONTINUOUS

Vance walks to that door. Hesitates. Puts her hand on it.

For the first time, she opens it — and goes in. We hold on the door as it closes behind her. We do not follow. Yet.

> **END OF ACT TWO**

====

> **ACT THREE**

INT. DECK 52 TOWER - CHARLES'S OFFICE - NIGHT

The cable-news segment plays, muted, on the wall. On screen: an ANCHOR in horn-rimmed glasses; behind her, two file photos — CHARLES and VAUGHN, twenty years younger, arms around each other at a launch party.

Charles watches his own history be told almost right.

On screen, the Anchor talks. And Charles — without any HUD, without any overlay, just a brilliant man watching a face — murmurs the next word out of her mouth a half-second before she says it. Then the one after. He is reading her the way he read a poker table at eighteen. He does it without triumph, the way you'd flinch at a habit you hate.

THE VOICE (V.O.)
He took the Navy contract to be seen. Within the week he'll realize the only person who'll believe him without laughing is the one he can't call.
(beat)
You don't need the body, Charles. You need the man who has it. You always did.

CHARLES
(to the empty room; to her)
And when he comes?

THE VOICE (V.O.)
That's the hand you haven't played in twenty years.

Charles turns off the broadcast. Sits in the dark with the face-down photograph.

INT. FLASHBACK - EMILY'S APARTMENT - NIGHT (18 YEARS AGO)

EMILY RHAIGE (20s) — luminous, formidable — hangs up a phone, absorbing a blow. Young Charles watches from the doorway, unsure whether to come in.

EMILY
(flat; not looking at him)
Didn't get it. Third callback, and they gave it to a name.
(beat)
Ask me if I'm okay. Go ahead. Watch what happens.

CHARLES
(carefully)
...Are you okay?

She turns — and she's not crying. She's already dealing cards onto the table, fast, a croupier's hands, thinking with them.

EMILY
No. And I don't have to be, and that's the part nobody tells you.
(dealing)
My grandmother dealt everything with this deck. Spades on Fridays, gin when somebody died. She used to say — life's a deck of cards. You don't get to pick the hand.
(looks up; fierce, clear-eyed, entirely her own person)
Only how you play it. I'm not quitting because I got a bad card, Charles. I'm going to sit here and play this hand until it's a good one. That's not hope. That's *stubbornness with a philosophy.*

Charles looks at her — not as a prize, as the smartest, most durable person he's ever met.

CHARLES
(quietly)
Deal me in.

EMILY
(the ghost of a smile; back in the fight)
Ante's the dishes. And you always lose the dishes.

BACK TO:

INT. DECK 52 TOWER - CHARLES'S OFFICE - NIGHT (PRESENT)

Charles in the dark, the ghost of her still on his face. He takes out EMILY'S DECK OF CARDS. Shuffles once — a perfect riffle, the loudest sound in the quiet office.

He deals four cards face up on the desk: a HEART. A DIAMOND. A CLUB. A SPADE.

He looks at them the way a man looks at children he's failed. Her stubbornness with a philosophy — built into a war machine.

INT. DECK 52 TOWER - PIERCE'S INTEL FLOOR - NIGHT

Pierce, late, alone. On her screens: the "HOUSE RULES" file, open. The two matching maps. She stares at the thing she's chosen to carry.

Her phone BUZZES. UNKNOWN NUMBER. She answers, wary.

PIERCE
Pierce.

INTERCUT - INT. FIST HQ - SECURE LAB - NIGHT

Benchley by the wall safe — which he's just opened. He's found a second folder behind the first, one Vaughn never showed him: OTHER INCIDENTS. Photographs. A dozen of them. Not one dead sailor. A pattern, worldwide, and his own boss sat on it and sent Benchley into a federal building to swear they'd "seen this once."

A man whose entire code is loyalty to honest command has just learned his command isn't honest. That's the thing that breaks his discipline. He dials before he can talk himself out of it.

BENCHLEY
Ms. Pierce. The man who beat you this morning.

PIERCE
(instantly sharp)
How did you get this line?

BENCHLEY
Same way you'd get mine.
(beat; the discipline cracking)
I stood in a federal building today and swore we'd seen this thing once. I just opened my own boss's safe. It's not once, Ms. Pierce. It's a dozen. All over the world. He knew, and he pointed me at that admiral like a loaded gun and didn't tell me what I was aimed at.
(beat)
I owe that man my life. I don't owe him a lie this big. So I'm calling the one person in that room today who still minded the difference between right and winning.
(beat)
Mind your house. There's weather coming, and neither of our bosses is telling us the truth about how much of it there is.

He hangs up before she can answer — a man appalled at what he just did. Pierce lowers the phone slowly.

She looks at her two maps — the vendetta she's protecting — and, for the first time, past them, to the dark beyond the window. To the weather.

PIERCE
(to herself)
...What do you have, Colonel.

> **END OF ACT THREE**

====

> **ACT FOUR**

INT. DECK 52 TOWER - THE INITIATIVE - NIGHT

We finally follow Vance inside. It's not a torture chamber. It's worse: it's a *clinic*. Clean. Kind-looking. Three occupied suites.

In the first: "TALLY" / IDRIS ACHEBE-LUND (41), a mild, tired man, a lemon drop clicking against his teeth. He's mid-screening — across from him, a DESPERATE FATHER (40s), a folder of a sick child between them.

Vance watches through the glass with SULLY.

SULLY
(low)
Tally reads the volunteers. Tastes whether they mean it. If they mean it, Spades takes them.

VANCE
And who reads Tally?

Inside, the Father finishes his plea. Tally sets down a pen, sucks the lemon drop, and — we see it cost him something — nods to the intake camera. *He means it.* The Father exhales in desperate relief. Tally looks like a man who just signed something.

INT. THE INITIATIVE - TALLY'S SUITE - CONTINUOUS

Vance enters after the Father's gone. Tally doesn't look up.

TALLY
Dr. Vance. You've never come down here.

VANCE
I signed a form eleven months ago that put a boy in my clinic with a failing arm. I've decided to start reading what I sign.
(beat)
That man. The father. Did he mean it?

TALLY
(a beat; the terrible gift)
Every word. That's the part nobody understands about this floor, Doctor. The volunteers aren't lying. They mean it completely.
(sucks the candy)
That's what makes it a sin. If they were being tricked, you could save them. You can't save someone from a bargain they'd make again.

Vance sits. Two people who hold the company's conscience from opposite ends.

VANCE
What did they give you? For being able to tell.

TALLY
(quietly)
I taste every lie anyone tells me. Every one. The waiter who says the kitchen's open. The doctor who says it won't hurt.
(beat)
My daughter's fourteen. She's started lying to me the way they do — small, ordinary, necessary. And I taste every one, and I keep my face still, because the alternative is telling her what her father is.
(beat)
I'm going blind, Doctor. It's in the fine print. Six years, they think. A blind man who can still taste every lie told in a dark room.
(finally looks at her)
You want to shut this place down. I can taste that that's true. I'm asking you not to do it slowly. Do it before the next father comes in and means it.

Vance holds his gaze. The whole moral engine of the series, in one room.

INT. DECK 52 TOWER - CHARLES'S OFFICE - NIGHT

Vance enters — the only leader who walks into the Dealer's silence without trying to fill it. Charles is at the desk, the four suit-cards still laid out.

VANCE
I went into the Initiative tonight.

CHARLES
(a beat; he knew)
I know.

VANCE
You drew a line, Charles. It runs, it doesn't grow. That line only holds while you're here to hold it. And you are not here.
(beat)
Cho pushes it a form at a time. I push back a form at a time. But you built a wall and then went to look at a photograph, and walls don't hold themselves.

Charles looks at her — the person in the company he most resembles, and most avoids.

CHARLES
(quietly)
Do you know why I keep it running, Mara? A thing I believe is a sin?

VANCE
Tell me. I've been guessing for a year and I hate my guesses.

CHARLES
(simply)
Because I did the math. The same math I did after Emily — I followed a chain all the way to its end.
(beat)
Something is coming. I have better intelligence than any government on Earth and it all says the same thing. And on the day it arrives, the question will not be whether the Initiative was righteous. It will be whether I put the fittest thing we could make between the sky and a sidewalk where someone's Emily is walking with a garment bag over her shoulder.
(beat)
I believe I'm committing a sin to prevent a worse one. I believe I'm damning myself with my eyes open. And I would rather answer for that than for the sidewalk.

Silence. Vance has her answer, and it's worse than every guess.

VANCE
(barely)
You can't run a welfare mandate and a human-experiment program in the same building without one of them being a lie. And I finally know which one.
(beat)
The welfare's the lie, Charles. I'm the lie. I'm the conscience you point to so nobody looks at the door down the hall.

CHARLES
(the truest, cruelest thing)
Yes.
(beat)
And I need you to keep being it. Because the day you stop, there's nothing between this company and what Cho wants it to be.

Vance stands there, holding the weight of being the alibi and choosing, visibly, to keep holding it. She picks up her knitting. The needles start again.

VANCE
(at the door; not turning)
When it comes, Charles. The thing in the sky.
(beat)
I hope she was worth what you're going to make the rest of us pay for her.

She leaves. Charles sits alone with four cards and a face-down photograph.

> **END OF ACT FOUR**

====

> **ACT FIVE**

INT. DECK 52 TOWER - EXECUTIVE FLOOR - NIGHT

Late. The floor near-empty. But the Court is here, drawn together the way people are drawn to a fault line — Pierce, Kané, Cho, Vance, in loose orbit, the day's losses between them.

KANÉ
So we lost the contract, and the Dealer did nothing about it — beautifully, as ever — and now the four of us stand in a dark office pretending we're not deciding what this company is while he's not looking.

PIERCE
(carefully)
We're not deciding anything, Idris.

KANÉ
Naomi. You've had that look since you walked in from Crystal City. You know something. You always know something first.
(beat)
What did Benchley's people find in that woman?

A beat. Pierce weighs it — how much to say, to whom.

PIERCE
Something that isn't from here.

The word lands in the room like a stone in a pond. Cho's head comes up. Even Kané goes still.

CHO
(quietly; not skeptical — hungry)
Define "isn't from here."

PIERCE
Chemistry that doesn't occur on Earth. Or in the solar system. In a healthy woman, on a US carrier, dead of internal burns that never touched her skin.
(beat)
Benchley called me tonight. Off the record. To tell me there's weather coming that neither Charles nor Vaughn is being honest about.

VANCE
(the healer's dread)
And what does that make us? The four of us, fighting over a company, over a program, over a dead man's grief — while something walks toward all of it?

CHO
(already somewhere else; the whiteboard word alive in her eyes)
It makes us early. It makes us the only company on Earth that knows to start building for it *now.*
(to the room; genuine, chilling clarity)
You're all mourning a contract. I'm looking at the first proof that everything I've been trying to build isn't ambition. It's *necessity.* Whatever did that to that sailor doesn't care about Earth's ethics committees. And a company that won't make the fittest thing it can will be erased by something that will.

KANÉ
(quietly; the hawk finding a new sky)
...Then we stop fighting Galloway.

PIERCE
(sharp)
We do not stop fighting Galloway.

KANÉ
Naomi — if there's a real enemy — a *real* one—

PIERCE
Then the man who funded Emily's killers to win a news cycle is exactly the man I want to watch closely while the world ends. Proximity, Idris. Not peace.

They fall silent — four people who just felt the ground tilt, and each reached for a different handhold.

And above them, on the glass wall, the muted news graphic still turns: the fanned deck, the two old friends' faces, the question WHO IS DECK 52?

INT. DECK 52 TOWER - CHARLES'S OFFICE - SAME TIME

Charles, alone, watches his Court through the interior glass — four brilliant people arguing in the dark, each certain they're the reasonable one.

He does not go to them.

THE VOICE (V.O.)
You could end their war with a sentence. Tell them what's coming. Point the whole company at it.

CHARLES
(watching them; quiet)
And the day I do that, I hand Cho the future, Kané the trigger, and Vance a reason to leave. No.
(beat)
Let them fight. A company at war with itself doesn't notice how afraid it should be. Their war is the anesthetic. I'll take it away when they need to feel the knife. Not before.

He turns from the glass. Crosses to the desk. Picks up the red-dress photograph — looks at it one long moment — and this time does NOT turn it face-down. He sets it upright, facing the room. Facing him. A man done hiding, at least from her.

CHARLES (CONT'D)
(to her; barely)
Watch me play this one, Em.

He doesn't shuffle. He doesn't monologue. He simply picks up the burner phone from the drawer, checks it, and pockets it — a man walking toward a decision, not away from one.

INTERCUT - INT. SANTIAGO - PRIVATE FORENSICS LAB - NIGHT (SAME TIME)

A refrigeration unit ticks. A red fault light blinks on — no one near it. A logged shipment marked FIST — EYES ONLY sits in the failing cold. A gloved hand we never see above the wrist lifts the case, calm, unhurried, and walks it out a service door into the dark.

Somewhere a clerical system will record a temperature failure. A sad little accident that embarrasses no one.

INT. DECK 52 TOWER - CHO'S CLEANROOM - PRE-DAWN

Cho, alone, gets an alert. A sealed transit case rides a cart to her bench — the manifest reads DAMAGED SHIPMENT / DISPOSAL. She cracks the seal. Inside, packed in vapor: a viable tissue sample. The thing FIST believes it owns.

She does not ask how. She never asks how. She looks at it the way she looked at the word on her whiteboard — like a door.

CHO
(soft; to no one; to the future)
...Hello.

SMASH TO BLACK.

THE VOICE (V.O.)
(in the black; soft; that cadence)
The Dealer had made his move. Now everyone at the table had to find out they'd been playing.

END TITLE CARD: DECK 52

> **END OF PILOT**
